#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40a70c8d-dc8e-52bd-a679-ffd6d1940451" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Function With a Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9b836c-fae2-50e0-a795-3a27cfdd5785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+a}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319421} {:answer/id #uuid "3492000c-c2fa-51a4-bcee-24c414d9ca52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=7x-a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319554} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3d6dcf-2ec9-5287-a8ef-e92f9f472281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x-7}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319812} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb04d9c-7b29-5181-b95e-06fc65228ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+7}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320017} {:answer/id #uuid "d00e46ee-e183-5993-a320-dcbaa9aa4eae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x-a}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccb04d9c-7b29-5181-b95e-06fc65228ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+7}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320017} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5d4e30f-32ac-5460-b456-38e5a87775f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "abfc488e-a974-5dbc-b40c-2f281295336b" :question/math-academy-id "q-35217" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=ax-7$, where $a$ is a non-zero constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=ax-7\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=ay-7\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =ay-7 \\\\ x+7 & =ay \\\\ \\frac{x+7}{a} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+7}{a}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ccc7bce-8234-5ee1-bd8b-2bcdd3fcbd07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319574} {:answer/id #uuid "574ba857-9f96-590d-abdc-bb096d802847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319648} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7a7fe3-5133-5ba3-baf2-2c6b2e00ce1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319916} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c8a647-b5b3-5af4-85b7-661bdc068c81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320074} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b296d9-adc9-527b-a4b4-ccaabe649e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ccc7bce-8234-5ee1-bd8b-2bcdd3fcbd07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319574} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e14ff5e-6a4f-5e24-8876-c8a24ddf7181" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1ffa1d84-420d-51e5-b5d5-2102976566da" :question/math-academy-id "q-35243" :question/problem "![](images/67/67b77d2d3798e87593c7dddeea93603fff36d9153f53248cc2dab295f4879321.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule and the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x))}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=3.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{2}$ is an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\frac{1}{3}$ does not change the sign of the limit. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2})={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40a70c8d-dc8e-52bd-a679-ffd6d1940451" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Function With a Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28a8e3f3-e6e5-59bd-9144-f9733223829f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax-11a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319519} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee8eb52-9b9f-56f1-9ecb-13190d21a579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319819} {:answer/id #uuid "b056fbea-f31f-565e-a6ee-a22a4a856c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319927} {:answer/id #uuid "b321e6e4-fc1e-54d2-9a1a-305a18b80a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax+11a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319939} {:answer/id #uuid "b495768c-d594-5351-a058-aad82b451854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=x+11a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28a8e3f3-e6e5-59bd-9144-f9733223829f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax-11a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319519} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5fa981-8f9f-5e72-b3b9-955186ce41e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40530ca3-e238-5640-94b0-1433afa01d34" :question/math-academy-id "q-35310" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{a}+11$, where $a$ is a non-zero constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{a}+11\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{y}{a}+11\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{y}{a}+11 \\\\ x-11 & =\\frac{y}{a} \\\\ a(x-11) & =y \\\\ ax-11a & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=ax-11a\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "185960eb-bfc4-5b04-9b8e-889e9fa563fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402404} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e16aa5-8900-58f3-8772-5eddc1069f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402405} {:answer/id #uuid "de3fec5a-67b7-579c-a809-55a0c1f4c2ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402406} {:answer/id #uuid "10adf48f-3c4e-533f-ba78-f17c436ead07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402407} {:answer/id #uuid "8d0475d5-57f8-5642-b3fc-c1fb1233e0f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7e16aa5-8900-58f3-8772-5eddc1069f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402405} :answer-field/id #uuid "181f4699-3530-5c7f-a1cf-43e09f53ac45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da57a6bc-d0db-59f4-81d7-c54fef1172ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-35379" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}+3).$" :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}+3) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\,(\\infty ) \\\\  & =-\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7739ff43-c68c-58ca-8bbe-3cf898ab85f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322081} {:answer/id #uuid "8d033687-0dda-5d24-a50d-81609e131df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322273} {:answer/id #uuid "8f052679-93df-563a-8c3a-49901beaf19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322283} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2a112f-a59c-5195-8734-7545ee263058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322736} {:answer/id #uuid "eefd406f-e3a9-51c3-9df4-f6d2a447cae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eefd406f-e3a9-51c3-9df4-f6d2a447cae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323031} :answer-field/id #uuid "661e0d23-dac0-58fe-aeb6-82bf0e4b60b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "544516cf-cdec-5de8-af07-1ff7e5be101e" :question/math-academy-id "q-36004" :question/problem "![](images/15/1597ad53471d0d9b44812c5caa31bcb160cfa150d1df9a9314447fa49e62ccff.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $x\\in [-6,0]$\n- $x\\in [-6,4)$\n- $x\\in [4,10)$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n- The given function is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in [-6,0]$ and $x\\in [4,10).$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/7c/7cb282e3fa66af801ff03816973d5f5f1d0cb41e39d08a8addd27d7aa9c5b9df.png)\n\n      \n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval $[-6,4]$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/00/000953609a7b3e13ad09473ead62f96aa895c7f94aa618884e69883595004fd4.png)\n\n \n\n Therefore, the correct answer is \"I  and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "014f6668-666f-5040-a144-715105254126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321160} {:answer/id #uuid "26c34079-b0a6-5fc3-9a77-ee93f052ff20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321452} {:answer/id #uuid "80353f56-f611-551a-b4e9-abf19a92d1af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322153} {:answer/id #uuid "968df320-cd19-54ac-b971-d412835fd3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322348} {:answer/id #uuid "f59bf802-b4bc-54f8-8889-7338410f2cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80353f56-f611-551a-b4e9-abf19a92d1af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322153} :answer-field/id #uuid "28b86894-2e1c-5167-ac3e-a4dd5b6514f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d31db013-e3f7-5fe2-aefa-8428da418673" :question/math-academy-id "q-36011" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b02c2e94e144935f0ff96846cd51b5a969fa8f884a7ca1d09abb231b7c945232.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible? \n\n- $x\\in (-4,-2)$\n- $x\\in [-2,3]$\n- $x\\in [-3,3]$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n- The given function is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-4,-2)$ and $x\\in [-2,3].$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/53/53fe88780b2ccb7522d2f1baa711162ec99300c2a2d4da29bba9ca532026cd07.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on  the interval $x\\in [-3,3]$  because some lines will  intersect the curve at more than one point.\n\n![](images/63/63cee4fc05ab17a8e69d9bfae578fa83c24d73e4677065a54a33a25e0871e718.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc794c7-cd0c-5ebc-aa11-9b82e72905fd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Limits of Piecewise Functions with a Gap in the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6489f85-c078-529a-bb89-256282a9a13d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402497} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee1227f-4f52-5de5-b34e-e560db110256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402498} {:answer/id #uuid "b65d528b-38e9-53d6-b9b7-00b8f1c5ac41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402499} {:answer/id #uuid "e70c9d48-daf4-5c24-8f93-c596a8791141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402500} {:answer/id #uuid "668245ac-4011-59ee-a836-e35d283d1eb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b65d528b-38e9-53d6-b9b7-00b8f1c5ac41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402499} :answer-field/id #uuid "9766008d-bd13-5a70-b8fc-147a47588ba7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65911f9a-fa00-54b8-9673-9586456d002e" :question/math-academy-id "q-36696" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<0 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The right-sided limit ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ \n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nApproaching $x=0$ from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100977 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0995bfd4-20b1-5f12-9d1d-fcb180a6291e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19ed57d1-1d91-544a-bdae-7940b7113ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321348} {:answer/id #uuid "877e8444-5a27-500f-a477-baed7db698bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322223} {:answer/id #uuid "964d5dce-dd1f-5775-a66c-aba0c11d0274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322343} {:answer/id #uuid "98de86f1-ea9d-579c-b52c-2a809a3accce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322361} {:answer/id #uuid "e14ed661-5e3a-5437-bf1b-eb0a7535207c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e14ed661-5e3a-5437-bf1b-eb0a7535207c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322907} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf487efd-f750-56a9-8fe4-aff8fa623557" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df0978e3-40c7-5749-aacd-3714af936e64" :question/math-academy-id "q-36720" :question/problem "Find the limit at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}6x+1\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -4{log}_{2}⁡x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<\\frac{1}{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=6x+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{-}}(6x+1) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow,  approaching from the right, we have $x>\\frac{1}{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-4{log}_{2}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}-4{log}_{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-4{log}_{2}⁡\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-4{log}_{2}⁡{2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-4(-1){log}_{2}⁡2 \\\\  & =-4(-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left- and right-sided limits are equal to $4,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100978 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdd4a2e4-b0e6-54e8-972c-3dc194ce4ab3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Do Not Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0331564f-2588-501b-b00b-4bfc21903eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321178} {:answer/id #uuid "26f8feb1-5ef3-5db8-83c3-0253bb71a162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321454} {:answer/id #uuid "30a31cb0-e610-5382-bc68-23ae87a10893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321520} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9a9190-a31e-5666-90db-d2259e210b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322279} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e36c4d-2ff0-5296-a228-00cad37c904e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26f8feb1-5ef3-5db8-83c3-0253bb71a162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321454} :answer-field/id #uuid "6db0b422-9f7f-5b42-ba98-b50b2a2c0d76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2777af2b-a5d2-5c98-9043-4dca19a2df59" :question/math-academy-id "q-36724" :question/problem "Find the limit at $x=3$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{3x-1}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi )\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<3,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{3x-1}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\sqrt[3]{3x-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{3\\cdot 3-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>3,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi ).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are different, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4caea11f-484f-50a4-a520-52df284329ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321753} {:answer/id #uuid "54e75cb9-e5b0-5ea8-9eaf-304e5de715df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321816} {:answer/id #uuid "888f7548-fc8c-5732-aae9-9e3aa70555ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322236} {:answer/id #uuid "c304d552-6f05-5067-a107-0c7b855a1c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322661} {:answer/id #uuid "f99f1c2f-bc18-5796-a3e2-28f9f9b5b60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "888f7548-fc8c-5732-aae9-9e3aa70555ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322236} :answer-field/id #uuid "9392c5de-5972-51d1-8356-6d7fe3ba1f53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e891cfb3-f978-5b44-b8bf-4d4f9f71b503" :question/math-academy-id "q-36941" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3cbdac583a4cdf1b5eb761892934516a0b4edd1487bd402ae0014859c4a0ad53.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-2,0]$\n- $x\\in (-2,2]$\n- $x\\in [-4,-2]\\cup [2,4]$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-2,0].$ Indeed, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/d7/d7c6e8d314483ad67e89ce5d4675b7af27fa08c88a4ed9d95eed9ad2a4da51cc.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-2,2]$ and $x\\in [-4,-2]\\cup [2,4]$ because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/ce/cee829f7e3d5f32b0acb54cb5c40406d8391d2a2967c68df0c1f5e1cae87d729.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100978 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdd4a2e4-b0e6-54e8-972c-3dc194ce4ab3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Do Not Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f56bdd5-a607-5af2-b88c-b12454f5dbac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392103} {:answer/id #uuid "f80ff926-3aa7-5c1c-b968-b56b246178c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392104} {:answer/id #uuid "544549f0-b759-5c27-aa3c-4223a9df60df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392105} {:answer/id #uuid "4fccaa0b-2b3d-58c3-881e-5c696c14c18c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392106} {:answer/id #uuid "8a29317f-e4cb-5886-99c5-3539d5b2e238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a29317f-e4cb-5886-99c5-3539d5b2e238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392107} :answer-field/id #uuid "04efa2b5-a248-5966-8fd4-c3f5d9dc1a1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14d10373-1baf-5303-9f9f-7dbe595ac72a" :question/math-academy-id "q-37488" :question/problem "Find the left-sided limit at $x=0$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x\\le 0 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8126b5-2054-5d88-865f-be6cbe674d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321368} {:answer/id #uuid "3466518b-1130-594a-9f91-8439ec16fa46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321544} {:answer/id #uuid "51eee8c3-23ea-5613-a452-4c2182222cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321794} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba4b8d7-052a-5cf2-86db-50636fe22d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321871} {:answer/id #uuid "c2131ada-4ea2-5f29-85f1-aa42dc442a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3466518b-1130-594a-9f91-8439ec16fa46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321544} :answer-field/id #uuid "617b107f-dc16-5899-aab5-ab0f5850cc8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9112180a-ac6b-5472-ba8b-819d346ead1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37915" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6c36dbce847c719accc77fa15f33dfb9a87cfae669691afe30f7693663076d2e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/52/52fe94bb303a001e89f9db221e9730d4ca8c5c284217aa20fb6084ee041dec66.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{10}{16}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{20}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "374ae646-8b4e-5b0e-bd7e-4acb5d1c0ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324420} {:answer/id #uuid "7d1612fc-e261-50ce-9313-fe26f50738e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325026} {:answer/id #uuid "c86a33ca-8732-5643-a35e-d2538c0e58e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325709} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e6cb1a-5418-5746-8b18-030e1c8789a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325813} {:answer/id #uuid "f0206e22-2c8b-5150-92b8-e52b0d52a598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "374ae646-8b4e-5b0e-bd7e-4acb5d1c0ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324420} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b0c2133-1c76-5099-a8ab-82d13698a8d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aa66e43c-92cc-5831-9942-3be250ef9ed8" :question/math-academy-id "q-38546" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fdb835ed2998fae4d8afc3fd5e593bac6c72b1ee45c8b4fe01bdc4348322d1e.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$\n- The function is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34aa8f4e-20f1-5dda-ac53-6eb411543897" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09148cf6-e660-5422-9fa7-a9f4738defd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402651} {:answer/id #uuid "3340c634-8f83-5193-ae75-2b37a7b6c946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402652} {:answer/id #uuid "f4481476-873e-5b4e-a202-210e8449c587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402653} {:answer/id #uuid "63b1b59c-8954-5973-ae03-cc16a86d080d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402654} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f0241d-3539-5ced-b138-fdde689dc4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3340c634-8f83-5193-ae75-2b37a7b6c946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402652} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f13134e-6c13-54bc-aaf2-8a968a13df74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4ced118-ede6-56c5-a59a-002b28515d4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-38580" :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea87f040e4249a04c21dd6c8466ffd194d60753056c29f00d8956e182db61ff3.png)\n\nThe graph of the polynomial $y=F(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The degree of $F(x)$ is odd.\nII. The degree of $F(x)$ is even.\nIII. The coefficient of the leading term is positive." :question/worked-solution "The end behavior of $F(x)$ is\n\n- $F(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ and\n\n- $F(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $F(x)$ is odd, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $F(x)$ is positive.\n\nSo II is false, and I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34aa8f4e-20f1-5dda-ac53-6eb411543897" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "520ce037-6d65-5a87-816c-38b9a9d22f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324642} {:answer/id #uuid "b137372b-d54b-51cb-8c70-5c8eaabe8e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325482} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ee0489-97b3-566f-8f0e-c62d506da27a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325704} {:answer/id #uuid "cb11e15c-7b44-5232-8e56-3e85080ab86c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325731} {:answer/id #uuid "e61483f3-87ce-5ef0-b035-b6a704d798b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b137372b-d54b-51cb-8c70-5c8eaabe8e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325482} :answer-field/id #uuid "ceb549ca-f772-54ab-ac92-58fb214b0833" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f40578d-2e88-5fde-bb73-48381fd621fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-38617" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb4959f437e11b767be136a2a024260d45ed29805cd57b1832d74d7cc2da11c1.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $y=P(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n- The degree of $P(x)$ is odd.\n- The coefficient of the leading term is negative." :question/worked-solution "The end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is even, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\nTherefore, statements I and II are false, while statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129144 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7cf112-ab08-5e7a-855d-91bb69ec7f76" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Polynomial Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fbb6282-911b-5b66-b192-305d440d6e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x^{6} + x^{4} - 5x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127562} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a044cc-7963-5872-b7e6-6d9a2f224fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-7x^{8} + 6x^{7} - 4x^{3} + 2x^{2} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127563} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb2454f-bf3e-5aa4-afce-9896ef787f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-5x^{5} + 3x^{2} + x + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127564} {:answer/id #uuid "3e5b9971-58f5-5d6d-9f95-7c155519b2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-x^{3} + 2x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127565} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0c079c-6769-57c1-b736-a89c4eef2463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x^{3} - x^{2} + x - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127566}] :answer-field/id #uuid "db6524ac-b4e5-521e-a137-1133e610aa7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127561} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb2ff6c4-35ee-5c86-88b6-f2b778bb9b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{3}+2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402657} {:answer/id #uuid "0cf46694-f517-57c1-b656-f30bd243fe5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-7{x}^{8}+6{x}^{7}-4{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402658} {:answer/id #uuid "0cabba40-fb5e-5284-8447-f1cf64e6820e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{6}+{x}^{4}-5x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402659} {:answer/id #uuid "e52d814a-f821-5e6e-934c-6f5a0f7ba906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402660} {:answer/id #uuid "343e1b78-b8cf-5288-841e-bb541f7dedd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5{x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}+x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb2ff6c4-35ee-5c86-88b6-f2b778bb9b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{3}+2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402657} :answer-field/id #uuid "702788a0-aadc-5877-8e1c-f586a9038e3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e1b36cf-56ef-56e7-80a1-3a4e3d34ad42" :question/math-academy-id "q-38624" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d873bd2af8e397d6c540267340b6105f413928c4279b939110aa4bb5b00d8948.png)\n\nWhich of the following polynomials could be the function shown in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is odd, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\n- the $y$-intercept is negative, which means that $P(0)<0.$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, only $P(x)=-{x}^{3}+2x-1$ satisties all these conditions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129144 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7cf112-ab08-5e7a-855d-91bb69ec7f76" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Polynomial Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ec8bba-61a9-5e53-9f83-ae6306a35d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{x}^{6}+\\sqrt{7}{x}^{3}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324133} {:answer/id #uuid "1d3e9e3c-8f63-5b55-a2b5-d6ae2f7cc85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324219} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6cc728-1b58-586c-b553-5f4c6de4cade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3{x}^{3}+8x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324575} {:answer/id #uuid "858b3ba7-0126-52d6-a92c-623111bcf464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{x}^{8}+4{x}^{2}+6x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325099} {:answer/id #uuid "d96579c4-211b-512e-819d-f2e5755afaf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6{x}^{5}+4{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d3e9e3c-8f63-5b55-a2b5-d6ae2f7cc85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324219} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c01c0f4-68e1-5f41-ac69-b50a572974f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c04700c1-06e0-50a6-8674-83b9d2f36915" :question/math-academy-id "q-38626" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb467eed4327c7feaa4cb0aad4495032fe52518891bb6d33227352c953ce4d35.png)\n\nWhich of the following polynomials could be the function shown in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is even, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\n- the $y$-intercept is positive, which means that $P(0)>0.$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the only one that satisfies all three criteria is $y=-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-x+2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42f76b4e-c2e3-5210-b8b9-db6bc53bafc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324510} {:answer/id #uuid "868d64bf-05a4-5a8a-bb2f-f215d1a3fb4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325114} {:answer/id #uuid "aefcef1a-2486-5ad4-889b-d4796ad4702f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325458} {:answer/id #uuid "dfae7751-a48a-56fb-8fe9-dabe433a0205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325924} {:answer/id #uuid "fd71fe36-ab9d-5173-8340-e63507c5a1eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "868d64bf-05a4-5a8a-bb2f-f215d1a3fb4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325114} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1d289fa-530e-56e8-81da-88ee4282c3b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbe22a70-053f-5354-8d7b-dc32dacb2db4" :question/math-academy-id "q-38770" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd19717bd41b787346a96394ce3bc4e38fe690340bb73bd3d11139d41ff977e2.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=2$ is a simple root.\n- $x=-1$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n- $p(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=2$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=-1$  has an even multiplicity because at $x=-1$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ moves right in the positive direction towards $+\\infty ,$ the $y$ value moves up in the positive direction towards $+\\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129302 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "503a2d0a-ba6c-5529-9f26-b83c769a26a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b52373-27a9-501e-8947-0c1f244a213e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324149} {:answer/id #uuid "5bec4237-30c8-54eb-a9f5-13ccaf205551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324732} {:answer/id #uuid "85ed53c0-2287-5169-b4e2-c59609989228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325105} {:answer/id #uuid "d964678d-9ceb-5d76-929a-65b4dd6a6052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325861} {:answer/id #uuid "df8ff16b-fe1f-5c14-b271-df5bace274b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df8ff16b-fe1f-5c14-b271-df5bace274b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325923} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9cd61a3-c73b-5511-815b-f6d529fa4ad2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45b870b0-b774-5b07-bef2-72f771e77f18" :question/math-academy-id "q-38999" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $y=\\frac{2}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083681 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "135f42a4-d1ee-5f2c-af3a-0c8aa0176bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324143} {:answer/id #uuid "268df909-c8fb-563f-af2f-c08134584f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324283} {:answer/id #uuid "69e770f2-5fac-5581-b92a-b1b948231e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324857} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3c6363-b72d-5a0d-9eec-918fa9888a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325152} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9d3f67-6d9d-5067-8456-4fe529f4d545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69e770f2-5fac-5581-b92a-b1b948231e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324857} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f2953d1-fb0d-5e3e-8ec9-21bd1672c52d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f572c08e-edf8-5f9d-9827-c106bc98ece4" :question/math-academy-id "q-39033" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}-15x-25$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-25$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-25}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 5,\\,\\pm 25,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{25}{3}\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ are $-1$ and $-\\frac{5}{3}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $3$ | $5$ | $-15$ | $-25$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-5$ | $0$ | $25$ |\n|  | $3$ | $0$ | $-15$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})({x}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})(x+\\sqrt{5})(x-\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-\\frac{5}{3},-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5}.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5}{3}-\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{5}=-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d1d0905-b59d-5d4a-909c-cea1b665a291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402677} {:answer/id #uuid "f2fa7f39-114c-5150-972f-6a6f4f47133a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402678} {:answer/id #uuid "77d63650-bd84-5a65-ad3f-110fff12b852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402679} {:answer/id #uuid "a04b8362-fdb8-5b5f-84e2-c7356b9ecb6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402680} {:answer/id #uuid "6d67d68d-f930-5fbf-a8ad-1dfef015cd8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d1d0905-b59d-5d4a-909c-cea1b665a291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402677} :answer-field/id #uuid "570ae7f1-bab1-51b3-a682-ac5497a6d1ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "494506ed-3be9-5fa5-a304-e992bf8e5303" :question/math-academy-id "q-3946" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(4{x}^{2}-5x+16).$" :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(4{x}^{2}-5x+16) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(4{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60c99086-75e7-5d91-b425-4e1b6f582bcc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce1c8713-2cf3-5d3e-be72-a3135dfaed78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1,\\,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402690} {:answer/id #uuid "fd5c07fd-aef8-50a6-bb7a-0214e75d1fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=1,\\,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402691} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d397dd-0706-56c3-a545-4f1e11b5d1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402692} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3acbb2-152b-5386-a5b3-d4d5f9778946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402693} {:answer/id #uuid "466aa8d5-5559-5bc5-8a5d-ddd22b7bb453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9d397dd-0706-56c3-a545-4f1e11b5d1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402692} :answer-field/id #uuid "aba4d313-1d11-58c4-bb5b-3d3a12220a0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca4d349e-c1ee-5a19-a1e0-b0c9e7e02dd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-39793" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x}{(x+3)(x+1)(x-1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)(x+1)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the three solutions $x=-3,$ $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are  $x=-3,\\,$ $x=-1$ and $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a602f803-9ee4-55ae-ba4f-d88ab6ec6a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402697} {:answer/id #uuid "d1acf715-1373-5a06-b01b-ab3b303c535d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=0,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402698} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbc63c9-13bb-57db-8074-636542eaac49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402699} {:answer/id #uuid "66e7f76d-26a7-52c7-993f-99c8712a83c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402700} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad6dfaf-76c0-5356-9336-dc60a8e77b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ad6dfaf-76c0-5356-9336-dc60a8e77b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402701} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4e77536-5db4-551c-9973-483a9864ffa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "10e486fb-a153-567a-b122-83eab72817c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-39936" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{4}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{({x}^{2}-1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{(x-1)(x+1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}}{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd0aa5fc-79de-525e-a688-a8a2d2fcf858" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Constant in a Transformed Reciprocal Function Given the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36817ff5-94b5-5e0c-aa6e-e4adfd7b868f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324418} {:answer/id #uuid "58722e71-13e2-5d86-b77b-8b1c37339129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324706} {:answer/id #uuid "796d99a4-c609-5611-b9b4-030174fd4671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324984} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb00e44-4f46-5766-841d-29d6d2a9f5b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325767} {:answer/id #uuid "dd1f23f3-9644-55d1-99f6-e9850f0217bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ceb00e44-4f46-5766-841d-29d6d2a9f5b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325767} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a286a71-fa21-5505-b96c-f7267c23eddf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d984b68-ba41-5abd-a3f2-afc368e8283a" :question/math-academy-id "q-40027" :question/problem "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4}{3x+b}$ is $(-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (-4,\\infty ).$ Find the value of $b.$" :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+b & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-b \\\\ x & =-\\frac{b}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=-\\frac{b}{3}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=-4$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $-\\frac{b}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{b}{3} & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{b}{3} & =4 \\\\ b & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129102 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd6703d2-36f6-5665-90e3-f75aea5f663b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Expression for the Domain of a Transformed Tangent or Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56259cef-6878-51cd-b1f5-eeaf49702140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324688} {:answer/id #uuid "654e271d-49cd-5e3c-9c7d-b37514a25a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324810} {:answer/id #uuid "8b458fe6-2549-577d-823d-3fcf10021ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325153} {:answer/id #uuid "d136e134-3728-57dd-b18c-c29e658d49ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325789} {:answer/id #uuid "fd810710-4cf3-54c8-88a7-9403d3f91aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{n\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "654e271d-49cd-5e3c-9c7d-b37514a25a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324810} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff7a4b9e-8f3f-5b3c-98a8-33205f758cf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a89fb0a0-3a36-552f-b729-6c5581cebcd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-41106" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})+1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})+1,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 3x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ 3x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3\\cdot 3}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115202 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc0be03-9369-508e-9765-8c88871ec20b" :knowledge-point/title "The Range of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87c6f1e6-0d33-5cdc-af67-5f19a9b4678a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325127} {:answer/id #uuid "cb12b821-bce4-53ba-809b-8073091be039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325732} {:answer/id #uuid "cf3e7d3c-28cb-551b-814d-24c71777a04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325772} {:answer/id #uuid "dbdb2d32-06a5-584c-b837-e088b83a23f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325886} {:answer/id #uuid "eb32a561-e406-5ef8-b8f6-32579968df6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87c6f1e6-0d33-5cdc-af67-5f19a9b4678a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325127} :answer-field/id #uuid "14aade30-4dcf-54fe-90ad-21a8d183cf5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d356ad64-d32b-5d91-870e-d983fd54ca81" :question/math-academy-id "q-41240" :question/problem "Determine the range of the function $f(x)=2{log}_{5}⁡(2x-1)+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of the function $y={log}_{5}⁡(x)$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068753 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8058df30-04de-555c-adfa-61fbf2b0ff31" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions in One Variable With Common Factors in the Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15017b89-6a83-59c3-8ca4-1e65d3e32c85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{1-2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324161} {:answer/id #uuid "3a903b7b-dcca-5c4f-8920-9a1546e6dcf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-14y+45}{5(1-2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324443} {:answer/id #uuid "c79526ea-db45-5cfa-a2b6-882255022a24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14y}{5(1-2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325697} {:answer/id #uuid "dc042986-d3e3-5167-8273-d4f156249300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26y-45}{5(1-2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325890} {:answer/id #uuid "e379b819-7fe0-521e-bbc1-1371d8e50ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y+9}{1-2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a903b7b-dcca-5c4f-8920-9a1546e6dcf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-14y+45}{5(1-2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324443} :answer-field/id #uuid "36e51594-6076-558b-b4c1-486d8c2bf0d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e010d20d-afd2-59fa-8a1c-50d451ce4776" :question/math-academy-id "q-41576" :question/problem "Given that $\\frac{6y}{5(1-2y)}=p$ and $\\frac{9-4y}{1-2y}=q,$ what is $p+q?$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to add the following fractions:\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{6y}{5(1-2y)}+\\frac{9-4y}{1-2y}\n$$\n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $5(1-2y)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6y}{5(1-2y)}+\\frac{9-4y}{1-2y} & = \\\\ \\frac{6y}{5(1-2y)}+\\frac{5}{5}\\cdot \\frac{9-4y}{1-2y} & = \\\\ \\frac{6y}{5(1-2y)}+\\frac{5(9-4y)}{5(1-2y)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6y+5(9-4y)}{5(1-2y)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6y+45-20y}{5(1-2y)} & = \\\\ \\frac{-14y+45}{5(1-2y)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129124 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5696c2d5-4e35-5ed3-a4a2-6fe9b158e461" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d1c4191-fab1-56ef-9a9e-5df4f5a37573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in [4,6]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324871} {:answer/id #uuid "705447cb-ac85-5790-aadf-544a4a2ca55d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in [-2,6]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324896} {:answer/id #uuid "c37b50d3-93b0-5e4b-a2f6-ad5ca8b620b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in [3,6]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325652} {:answer/id #uuid "d9fbb5f9-1876-576d-956d-164f993762da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in [2,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325870} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d1017a-3133-56bf-ad84-e9bd0f77a8d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in [2,6]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3d1017a-3133-56bf-ad84-e9bd0f77a8d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in [2,6]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325976} :answer-field/id #uuid "5600e685-5275-5ef3-a75f-13f121192c83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8be6063d-ee1d-5aea-9fe3-f1f6572dff56" :question/math-academy-id "q-41840" :question/problem "What is the range of the function $y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})+4?$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-2$ and then adding $4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})\\le 1 \\\\ 2 & \\ge -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})\\ge -2 \\\\ 6 & \\ge -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})+4\\ge 2 \\\\ 2 & \\le y\\le 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y\\in [2,6].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129082 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c61dc7c0-d3f8-5a52-a767-d5a5950ed61b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ =-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128132} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3acfbd-450b-529e-9e81-f6898d0afc15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ =-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128133} {:answer/id #uuid "425120b8-9a7a-509b-b2b1-16f549aee0d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ = \\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128134} {:answer/id #uuid "51495d9a-03f0-5f49-aeab-3c6ea5811fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128135} {:answer/id #uuid "681b517b-c0da-555d-9464-011b10f62e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ = \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128136}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a1f2f1dc-9d19-53e5-af38-cc9ea5f6ad94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128131} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2ab46d8-4b20-5cb0-bd03-d4843745957e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402771} {:answer/id #uuid "1281daa1-21d2-521b-ba7b-2f63be883923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402772} {:answer/id #uuid "cf49d4c9-aad2-5d7f-bca3-cd2582a8d854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402773} {:answer/id #uuid "1efc176a-2f08-5690-b21d-dbaa2aa9e6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402774} {:answer/id #uuid "9257c9b4-8a20-5a75-b76e-4b9c11a339a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1efc176a-2f08-5690-b21d-dbaa2aa9e6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402774} :answer-field/id #uuid "737f5b1b-3e66-5582-9362-63911dfedfe9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "299c91a4-6289-5d24-ace1-495b52634a62" :question/math-academy-id "q-42043" :question/problem "![](images/91/919b9e8f2a9493fd0dda3e0d7bf66de9af054fc4ed9c0fcf7a68329fdbbaffdd.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2},$ what is the value of $tan⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/94/948610f67267e6ec1b845d2b4e1bc4030b81a1080bcb9a44d9f974ff78c3f7b0.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096652 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c24834-8cd4-5bea-b65d-b2c5663746e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327004} {:answer/id #uuid "43f90f38-cbf5-5d7a-920b-1121b5764f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =0$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327528} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7ece4b-a049-56ac-8617-bdfeb30b3311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328419} {:answer/id #uuid "b21ee6fb-0023-5a59-b1cc-92b08b1e5bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328478} {:answer/id #uuid "baf8f242-62cb-5b3c-a8e9-9e47509ff9d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08c24834-8cd4-5bea-b65d-b2c5663746e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327004} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce77403f-2bf1-5fb6-9d50-de0552a7684f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f6563bc-e431-538e-8c1d-8eaa7d0e394f" :question/math-academy-id "q-42657" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dc708da9adb4f61161ee1face89403bf1a6ce9762905e8a59b4b3ed9031c817a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/84/84f8951c4921eca20b00132996dc7b2dfafe047d58574e1822da795e1444c861.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=3|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ has the opposite direction to $p$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=-3p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ has the opposite direction to $q$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=-2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =-3p-2q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=-3p-2q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096653 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3b19d7-9caa-5865-b36b-62dba7ca1b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{1}{3}u+\\frac{2}{3}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327054} {:answer/id #uuid "12bc31bd-410e-5cec-b3e8-db5ec16e6668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}v-\\frac{2}{9}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327095} {:answer/id #uuid "1d8032c3-8159-562d-b85e-ce01f3a9419c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327189} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd9827b-fe26-5576-843d-788fca86322f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{2}{3}u+\\frac{1}{3}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328732} {:answer/id #uuid "f6116bad-8f2c-5913-918c-8429cac9d1d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=-\\frac{1}{3}v+\\frac{2}{3}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d8032c3-8159-562d-b85e-ce01f3a9419c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327189} :answer-field/id #uuid "624e21c0-b503-5eed-93a7-5e549f2e0d0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfe5446c-2b26-5e48-974b-de364027f648" :question/math-academy-id "q-42669" :question/problem "![](images/04/040127594f639248736d4492f1853bcb1e6ef18c37db9242ebe466045713628a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OV}=v$ and $W$ divides $\\overset{―}{UV}$ in the ratio $UW:WV=2:7.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OW}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{UW}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OUV,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =\\overset{\\to }{UO}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-u+v \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $UW:WV=2:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{UW}=\\frac{2}{9}\\overset{\\to }{UV}=\\frac{2}{9}(v-u).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OW} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =u+\\frac{2}{9}(v-u) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176051 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2a8cf2-64db-5b2f-b0f7-0c727cf492a9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a3b4306-55a9-5f15-98c8-f5d60b6a40ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327443} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf0eaf4-db08-533b-aed6-515990875a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327466} {:answer/id #uuid "72c7c4d8-6511-5726-b3e9-0656b209f8f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327928} {:answer/id #uuid "90fc077c-9a61-5c22-a6a7-607c04c3efe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328200} {:answer/id #uuid "95047154-213d-51aa-9ff0-8ef74040020d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90fc077c-9a61-5c22-a6a7-607c04c3efe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328200} :answer-field/id #uuid "40a9794c-8ea9-5381-b6f9-0d109bd0dcbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3923a242-a394-5f37-9f6d-741dafd435c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-42886" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=sec⁡x?$\n\n- The point $(0,2)$ is in the graph\n- $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote\n- $y=-2$ belongs to the range of the function." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/da/da729794a0331383debd75be0a4ce4a9f2d4da3286f50bd4f284868996077412.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The $y$-intercept of $sec⁡x$ is $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $y=sec⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ thus $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote.\n\n- Statement III is true. The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ so $y=-2$ belongs to the range."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec62fd08-5729-5dc4-97b9-1a422f70109e" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Expanding Parentheses and Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f4dfaf9-6adc-58e2-af62-11084821e994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327358} {:answer/id #uuid "88ee277f-7d39-51d6-ae8e-804455cc5483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328112} {:answer/id #uuid "9908e0fd-b391-508e-8dd9-9f1b7572eaab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328256} {:answer/id #uuid "b457a22e-59a5-554f-b4d6-d662134d1479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328503} {:answer/id #uuid "d0528873-0750-5fae-828d-e2dd5c23f87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f4dfaf9-6adc-58e2-af62-11084821e994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327358} :answer-field/id #uuid "a085cb5f-e013-5736-9ee5-2fef11903a5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "770be8a8-209f-5a4a-8f5b-267370ed95fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-43288" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059643 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1bc8c875-3393-5fd6-90cf-87737e16f2f2" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Trigonometric Expression Using a Common Denominator and the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d51a21-90c5-5b54-a6e4-e0b0b8a2ed02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327134} {:answer/id #uuid "40320fe3-c500-5dcb-a7c9-96f211e3f18e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327498} {:answer/id #uuid "7a98372a-b734-5075-bb5b-a84d747704c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}⁡t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327984} {:answer/id #uuid "95f2320a-4032-50dc-aeeb-456b3b00cb5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328238} {:answer/id #uuid "ace65715-85a6-5e2d-ba3b-d82ca77a9505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40320fe3-c500-5dcb-a7c9-96f211e3f18e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327498} :answer-field/id #uuid "34fb1090-54c7-5d88-a697-5bc7387e677b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db749ad3-a154-5aac-9219-0c39bfac704c" :question/math-academy-id "q-43298" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{1}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e85a2d68-d49f-5bf4-ab62-13ad8c35b0d4" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expression Using an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d9790e4-77cb-5ff4-ac43-39c78331e808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327191} {:answer/id #uuid "9939c042-c8aa-5188-bbb4-1c3c3f7a7f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328257} {:answer/id #uuid "a407281c-48ae-50b3-a5ba-d986468525b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328354} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f911ba-6e39-5c15-993b-525d98cd4ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328397} {:answer/id #uuid "e51dd51b-095f-57d7-a137-944306de7d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a407281c-48ae-50b3-a5ba-d986468525b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328354} :answer-field/id #uuid "37d7ae64-5756-5393-953b-b3fac5aef0ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3eb8e400-2919-528a-91cb-5d1221ea0b97" :question/math-academy-id "q-43308" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )?$" :question/worked-solution "First, use the identity $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ) & =(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we expand the parentheses in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105904 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cafdb077-0e02-5e7a-bf78-c3a8aacfb6f7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Angle or Side of a Triangle Given the Area of a Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55cfe684-dc18-5ab1-b164-6d50fade618a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327676} {:answer/id #uuid "8d23ee9d-454e-5907-a98d-d759e11996ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328151} {:answer/id #uuid "b7359851-720e-5ab4-876a-6265d25f515d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328528} {:answer/id #uuid "cf531d87-8a79-5162-b024-e25dc07d7e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328757} {:answer/id #uuid "e7164a97-e526-5ef2-8ced-7e38b414e248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf531d87-8a79-5162-b024-e25dc07d7e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328757} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbc9b871-5875-5e5c-8a91-d0aec6bf5882" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55d7cd50-6834-55b6-a52f-4f6d251b49dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-43339" :question/problem "![](images/85/85b13f2236c5f00da34ea71fdbfc967882fa6e66c347f6ef40c0604391a5d68e.png)\n\nThe area of ​​the triangle $△ABC$ is $15{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$ Find $m∠A.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=5\\,\\text{ cm}, \\\\ b & =AC=12\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $m∠A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 15 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot 5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 15 & =30\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{15}{30} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡A & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ m∠A & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129122 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1eeb42af-74ee-53d7-af9f-be171f77968f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f720e8d-bb1b-59ca-9ab6-dc161291bf7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327071} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc7ca04-3dfc-5466-a866-a199437da7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327775} {:answer/id #uuid "7851f496-18f4-5ce9-b27a-0a6147c6176e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327963} {:answer/id #uuid "85974f52-6385-50ae-a81b-e8bc2b19d899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{15}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328085} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb14292-bdff-5641-bc24-4dffec7b4e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7851f496-18f4-5ce9-b27a-0a6147c6176e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327963} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2a5f161-27b6-5dd4-aa10-c1cb6123c7a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72d5418e-aa83-5e26-b56d-9365871c6a6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-43411" :question/problem "What is the period of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x+{90}^{∘})?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is ${360}^{∘}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=8.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{8}={45}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae4289af-e251-514c-981e-5c6d21992ad3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Perpendicular Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e922a42-6deb-5bc6-a4f2-a8d06f3fea60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328024} {:answer/id #uuid "d59f7611-aece-5245-9299-86b545be9496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328803} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0e28cc-1799-536e-bd55-0a06dc16bb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328867} {:answer/id #uuid "de5640a4-5333-5d9b-b991-e89e51fe95ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-6x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328881} {:answer/id #uuid "ed73643a-713f-5f7a-aea6-c63401f50949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d59f7611-aece-5245-9299-86b545be9496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328803} :answer-field/id #uuid "2512d590-4b14-5e67-bd6c-bb6c4cebbb08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36e2ce70-a218-5096-a57b-5f93338562ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-44337" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9c426e75153bd893e3dbef39bd8a837991d8c3ec6a48b41aeb27df6c4250660.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the equation of the line ${l}_{1}$ is $y=2x-1$ and ${l}_{2}$ is perpendicular to ${l}_{1}.$ What is the equation of the line ${l}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $2,$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and a $y$-intercept of $6.$ Therefore, it can be written in slope-intercept form as\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110600 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e732f270-1d6f-5ff8-8792-d9b329ea858d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Standard Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04aa8a38-9ecc-5e87-9899-fa2c52620615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326982} {:answer/id #uuid "2a0d4423-cdbd-59bc-9519-c02229ca242c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327296} {:answer/id #uuid "842c8b78-fb24-5dfb-9556-59ef1eff7a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{5}x-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328076} {:answer/id #uuid "8f307607-46e6-546c-b68f-66ba5c7a0d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{5}x+15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328184} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b36271-f849-5b31-8979-398e7e127d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04aa8a38-9ecc-5e87-9899-fa2c52620615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326982} :answer-field/id #uuid "637a8d42-8d66-59a6-9b11-fb936c324619" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8ebc497-48a2-5b22-a14f-221db06dd5d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-44404" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $x+5y=10?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $x+5y=10,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5y & =10 \\\\ 5y & =-x+10 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{5}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=5x-4$ has a slope of $5.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot 5=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=5x-4$ is perpendicular to the given line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110601 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f3d7d62-686b-59c4-9ea1-695c6be3180b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Unknown Parameters Given Two Perpendicular Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c5cea50-fecb-5d5b-8a89-f4f34d327f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328005} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3ca05e-2e03-5005-8439-1e9b0e51d16a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328028} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d0efcf-7b5d-51ae-a082-657d77e63c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328768} {:answer/id #uuid "df927e48-fbac-512c-b73b-41214fbca6fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328892} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e47ca3-6ef9-5cec-9235-9aa609798010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f3ca05e-2e03-5005-8439-1e9b0e51d16a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328028} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e4084a6-ffaf-52c0-9e02-59ab5e2b3839" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa337919-23eb-5736-a04b-9af0d92af77d" :question/math-academy-id "q-44407" :question/problem "The lines $4x-8y=7$ and $3y-px=9$ are perpendicular. The value of $p=$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of $4x-8y=7,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-8y & =7 \\\\ -8y & =-4x+7 \\\\ 8y & =4x-7 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of $3y-px=9,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-px & =9 \\\\ 3y & =px+9 \\\\ y & =\\frac{p}{3}x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The slope of the line $4x-8y=7$ is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of the line $3y-px=9$ is $\\frac{p}{3}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{p}{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,p=-6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110602 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6fee8d5-e1f7-599d-a74c-abc44d973065" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Horizontal and Vertical Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a5593e-d6ed-5927-b3a1-d1daf628097e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327122} {:answer/id #uuid "30c1bcff-f9d0-5c30-91f5-4ef435a4a0c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327366} {:answer/id #uuid "76680e20-3119-50e8-b90e-149dee6da095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327947} {:answer/id #uuid "9aeb19e0-e051-568b-87f4-243574507bc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328276} {:answer/id #uuid "b5707a21-7927-57dc-8dfb-375cdb25c534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15a5593e-d6ed-5927-b3a1-d1daf628097e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327122} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1cd0532-96ff-5c54-9e68-ab598a48b1e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30c4eaa9-084c-53b2-8078-d461a7041d34" :question/math-academy-id "q-44691" :question/problem "Determine the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-7,-9)$ and is perpendicular to $y=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the line $y=5$ is a horizontal line, a perpendicular line would need to be vertical. So, the perpendicular line must take the form $x=k$ for some value of $k.$\n\nThe perpendicular line needs to go through the point $(-7,-9),$ which has an $x$-coordinate of $-7.$ So, the perpendicular line is $x=-7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b3ff37f0-6e45-5da4-ac87-1b93c8df3e80" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Solutions of Compound Inequalities on Number Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2430143-e3ef-56c0-9f1f-964a44b1fe36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/91/91ec8574742229a2c7fd5af8920f9137e2b25e28a6f02a7cf1edcc8a4db4b6d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392570} {:answer/id #uuid "42638bec-e49e-5814-a427-d1911ca95c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1a3b83780bbd15328077dc45eef965aaf311e704990ee3cb45078d76c96183a2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392571} {:answer/id #uuid "304db39b-9fd8-57ff-a43e-494c6882a179" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c8/c8c019b0d7a2b34e9791394ce1ac50f8db22b1d7fb9296a8c119a80a583d63ef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392572} {:answer/id #uuid "2102c409-a8fd-5bbe-a29f-a0866257c255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d3/d3ff8ace8f19f48631b57968c7aad223613e65f5be6f67a3196bd5fa9158ae51.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392573} {:answer/id #uuid "95f7974a-4774-510c-92d0-7a4e59b60df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5a/5a8e25a0a97ec193574fd8099e2dfdce70ed887e9f527b855b1ca0957ae7885b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2430143-e3ef-56c0-9f1f-964a44b1fe36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/91/91ec8574742229a2c7fd5af8920f9137e2b25e28a6f02a7cf1edcc8a4db4b6d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392570} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe31165c-2ea1-57a8-ac77-e3f03a131397" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e996239f-cafe-55a4-9e1e-af5edfdca48f" :question/math-academy-id "q-44761" :question/problem "Which of the following number lines gives the solution to $-1<-5x-6<4?$" :question/worked-solution "We isolate $x$ by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <-5x-6<4 \\\\ -1+6 & <-5x-6+6<4+6 \\\\ 5 & <-5x<10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we need to divide by $-5.$ However, because we are dividing by a negative, we also need to flip the signs of the inequalities.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & <-5x<10 \\\\ \\frac{5}{-5} & >\\frac{-5x}{-5}>\\frac{10}{-5} \\\\ -1 & >x>-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution can be written as $-1>x>-2$, but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers: \n\n$$\n-2<x<-1\n$$\n\nWe can represent this solution using a number line, as follows.\n\n![](images/91/91ec8574742229a2c7fd5af8920f9137e2b25e28a6f02a7cf1edcc8a4db4b6d5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129082 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0697fb32-13a8-5152-929c-1cbc92697a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326990} {:answer/id #uuid "29cbd9c5-1f6a-5f93-bfe0-6e1833448eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327292} {:answer/id #uuid "5f59d2b0-87ec-535c-98db-46f721956829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327771} {:answer/id #uuid "875b0e21-3bdb-537e-83a7-6070520501cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328098} {:answer/id #uuid "8c78c851-07e7-51bc-a294-83ea44fc4afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29cbd9c5-1f6a-5f93-bfe0-6e1833448eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327292} :answer-field/id #uuid "6067e08a-a253-5424-8dcb-5049856ea945" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c5295f76-3e41-52a4-88d5-2769f2995c21" :question/math-academy-id "q-45875" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\pi $ then what is the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "As $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}$ then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nLet's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/aa/aaba5af0542d29ef91d4b0e17907a81e32bccc402c48b32c205e7298630b5bc0.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$ we have:\n\n- $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(-\\frac{4}{5})}{(\\frac{3}{5})}=-\\frac{4}{3}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{5})}{(-\\frac{4}{5})}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{3}{4}=-\\frac{25}{12}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078557 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "045722b6-b558-522b-86e1-322c64e2848f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0801cf1c-5fd2-5cc2-8874-bb033ff63b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326999} {:answer/id #uuid "21618e1a-d4b1-5642-ac4b-4adec6bcb616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327222} {:answer/id #uuid "2a3ed060-8f3d-5e5f-a548-ad4adb40f6c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327301} {:answer/id #uuid "3f5696e8-74ce-57d4-98a1-81edbd390bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327489} {:answer/id #uuid "9d7d71e6-17cd-53a0-9ad8-987841eae685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a3ed060-8f3d-5e5f-a548-ad4adb40f6c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327301} :answer-field/id #uuid "914d0201-289f-570f-b5ca-55e4260ae1f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "005f9ab9-467f-5576-9116-815c97776952" :question/math-academy-id "q-46830" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-4}{\\sqrt{3x-3}-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=4$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-4}{\\sqrt{3x-3}-3} & =\\frac{4-4}{\\sqrt{3(4)-3}-3}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-4}{\\sqrt{3x-3}-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{(\\sqrt{3x-3}-3)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{{(\\sqrt{3x-3})}^{2}-{3}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{3x-3-9} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{3x-12} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{3(x-4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x-3}+3}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(4)-3}+3}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+3}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329796 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078536 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f3b50a7-1a31-52bc-91a4-f6b268955f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327070} {:answer/id #uuid "10710463-0db1-5838-be7b-4f17aca898c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327081} {:answer/id #uuid "196dae27-2242-5069-9416-9d2435e009f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327154} {:answer/id #uuid "519f1ddd-7a1e-5d1a-a24e-d9445baa5f01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327645} {:answer/id #uuid "fe2ff551-9a15-5bb4-987f-b343fdf4c92b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "519f1ddd-7a1e-5d1a-a24e-d9445baa5f01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327645} :answer-field/id #uuid "3556b158-4519-5e67-be7a-f8d92924db68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83a2f35e-6a3d-5f89-a27d-35edfe302559" :question/math-academy-id "q-46831" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2+2}-2}{{2}^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+2}-2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x+2})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x+2-4}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x-2}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{1}{(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2+2)(\\sqrt{2+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124671 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e37b91f0-ea0a-58b0-98d0-8f3626578dd6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit of Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3552e823-ed7e-5411-8a4d-a7a161d920bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327395} {:answer/id #uuid "3f22fb2d-15a6-595d-8e20-54b09e482167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327487} {:answer/id #uuid "6874f84d-9c2d-5c6c-8e62-74ddd0d5cfc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327835} {:answer/id #uuid "7b8ddd5a-8d1e-5b11-a6bc-864708f334f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327996} {:answer/id #uuid "bc89a2bc-ead2-5238-9aae-15a92a90fa81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f22fb2d-15a6-595d-8e20-54b09e482167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327487} :answer-field/id #uuid "10bd8af9-359c-5678-a39c-41fc93bcc176" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5898d1b3-7352-5733-948f-e0e93cd0becf" :question/math-academy-id "q-47039" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x).$\n\n![](images/5e/5e9d0e9d944cda9c97555ffdeac5aa245905f800cd99e11f5d172203bad3892d.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $\\pi $ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)$ are negative and decrease without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)=-\\infty \n$$\n\nHowever, as $x$ approaches $\\pi $ from the right, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)$ are positive and grow without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)=\\infty \n$$\n\nSince the two limits are not equal, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6835b52b-adaa-5c66-9f6d-d32591864659" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Infinite Limits Using the Laws of Exponents and the Algebra of Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17028eac-4ec5-5412-a437-179e2341db73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327136} {:answer/id #uuid "699925f0-6e8c-5f1a-b77d-e1c6f323fb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327842} {:answer/id #uuid "aa0370f2-56b3-5cc8-a52d-9b4a98865995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328406} {:answer/id #uuid "cdfe3a0e-970e-5bb9-8a35-e018230647f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328745} {:answer/id #uuid "f5621511-1f8d-5799-9acc-c62ea19974de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdfe3a0e-970e-5bb9-8a35-e018230647f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328745} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f990210-b8a8-5e34-9486-0ad7ae987df5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "66cb54a9-7be0-554a-a6fe-eb1aaa1b6f8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-47073" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x-8}=$" :question/worked-solution "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x-8} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x}\\cdot {2}^{-8} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{({2}^{3})}^{x}\\cdot {2}^{-8} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{8}^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{256}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{256})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{8}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={8}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/85/85319605a7c7b57d4c7ed29b8d409e3169f3331775580b91b70e2be99fef7986.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{8}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{256})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{8}^{x}=(\\frac{1}{256})\\cdot 0=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092240 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10131252-4695-56ed-8c59-747e545f594c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392584} {:answer/id #uuid "e6984861-e3cb-5365-9e9e-1ecd4311dcb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392585} {:answer/id #uuid "859687f9-c59b-551f-aaa5-e7e3c04ba6c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392586} {:answer/id #uuid "95424aa7-6153-5c48-9071-e6e5ac4c3b4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392587} {:answer/id #uuid "41016988-d005-506a-b687-2940e1c88cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95424aa7-6153-5c48-9071-e6e5ac4c3b4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392587} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9549a2f-75a6-5fa7-b3e3-19c27b3b484f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "414ee72f-747c-573a-9cad-a9cbdad6101a" :question/math-academy-id "q-47410" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=3{x}^{2}+4x$ at the point $(1,7)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+4x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}+4x)=6x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,7):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=6(1)+4=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(7) & =-\\frac{1}{10}(x-1) \\\\ 10y-70 & =-x+1 \\\\ x+10y & =71.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p+q=10+71=81.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "298a6c80-4207-50f6-8a34-1d93a5f89fed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327287} {:answer/id #uuid "307e57c3-1572-5e33-a5f9-3472b90bf91f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327363} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ee7da7-a08a-5f34-86ea-f3f106a5f98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328912} {:answer/id #uuid "e33c1589-993a-5e15-9743-78a5a9f95009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328926} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9c839b-8755-5fd8-b8d8-b4929a06d14c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb9c839b-8755-5fd8-b8d8-b4929a06d14c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329142} :answer-field/id #uuid "197f2e71-4c00-5a81-830b-269b6b29fd90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "704dafc6-9839-55e3-9683-dc5e275270d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-47449" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{2}+x$ at the point $(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}+x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =2x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=2(1)+1=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176051 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2a8cf2-64db-5b2f-b0f7-0c727cf492a9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "029e14a6-a3da-5d56-bd50-24aa3841ff59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326964} {:answer/id #uuid "09838c12-324b-5f86-b8eb-b9a50a5b99f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327012} {:answer/id #uuid "58d2b82d-ca43-5016-b810-dc807085c710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327713} {:answer/id #uuid "d9fe1f38-597e-53c5-aa82-041838d9bba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328838} {:answer/id #uuid "ed61f5fa-bf44-559a-b259-d059d2ed706f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed61f5fa-bf44-559a-b259-d059d2ed706f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329011} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc0b4dd2-5359-5754-b6be-a086a2742846" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ce070dc-f77b-529a-a508-4630f3613570" :question/math-academy-id "q-47455" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=sec⁡x?$\n\n- The period of the function is $\\pi .$\n- The function is increasing in the interval $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$\n- The function has no zeros." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/77/779d7a31646a229492a8165c8212a5c83ad7d37d843c1c1b3b36766a74bc5124.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The period of the function is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function $y=sec⁡x$ is decreasing on $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has no zeros."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096621 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c547746-49c8-5a4a-85fe-c6b75493a354" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24cdb15d-92a4-57bb-b3ae-70ee6398efed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}sin⁡x-cos⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327252} {:answer/id #uuid "28cffc72-f500-56eb-b62d-297789e3d352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327280} {:answer/id #uuid "537f4f22-dc76-5ecc-941c-28735a7fbb3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}sin⁡x-cos⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327658} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb04a5b-8198-547f-9f85-6d03684ff103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(sin⁡x-cos⁡x)}{cos⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327873} {:answer/id #uuid "6ebf154b-78ed-5323-9bc6-5af4e4ce0e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(sin⁡x-cos⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28cffc72-f500-56eb-b62d-297789e3d352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327280} :answer-field/id #uuid "1139e71c-df1c-5f00-8bad-e71886ea7ae5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ace0fb1-1a71-5cfd-952c-d11857b972c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-47943" :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{cos⁡x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=cos⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot cos⁡x-{e}^{x}\\cdot (cos⁡x)^{′}}{(cos⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}cos⁡x+{e}^{x}sin⁡x}{(cos⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "711f198b-26b4-5185-812a-102329d49313" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With the Tangent Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026a56b8-1115-5684-ac6e-ef1630e64970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326960} {:answer/id #uuid "0393bd6c-c00c-5794-bb05-1c8299be09b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2{sec}^{2}⁡\\sqrt{x}}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326972} {:answer/id #uuid "25ff194a-cfcd-58a0-adba-8bc91fdf1efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2x{sec}^{2}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327262} {:answer/id #uuid "44d191be-38a4-500e-8cfb-76a9d83fe2e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2\\sqrt{x}sec⁡x}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327534} {:answer/id #uuid "9336f84c-bb60-54b1-ba79-495700d341f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9336f84c-bb60-54b1-ba79-495700d341f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328218} :answer-field/id #uuid "39c98579-7ff9-5677-8870-c03daabb35df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db3973ed-197d-5377-b8db-60e027e16ad0" :question/math-academy-id "q-47983" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=\\sqrt{x}+tan⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+tan⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+{sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059675 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0de0627b-2568-5c02-8928-d14344df5a2f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With Sine or Cosine Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58178d4f-4abd-58b7-80f3-235da8563710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2sin⁡x-2{x}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327707} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8ba3a3-8163-54a7-a89c-617f3758ea02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2sin⁡x-2{x}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327890} {:answer/id #uuid "abf48582-4f98-5a96-ba7c-6645540d8053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2sin⁡x-2{x}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328424} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c3ad30-bf9c-5740-9f1f-85721ef68822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2sin⁡x+2{x}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328543} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5d65e6-8cdc-5871-afa6-4d5b3c079a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2sin⁡x-2{x}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abf48582-4f98-5a96-ba7c-6645540d8053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2sin⁡x-2{x}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328424} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa72fd76-960d-5bcb-9cf5-7f47b8602241" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f32d0b6b-0338-505d-81ba-ba8c8d1669de" :question/math-academy-id "q-47985" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=-2cos⁡x+{x}^{-2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(cos⁡x)=-sin⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2cos⁡x+{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =-2(-sin⁡x)+(-2){x}^{-2-1} \\\\  & =2sin⁡x-2{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329849 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096621 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c547746-49c8-5a4a-85fe-c6b75493a354" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d04b0f4-3b5e-5a83-ab7c-fadd2fae0aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xcos⁡x+2sin⁡x}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327753} {:answer/id #uuid "79438a62-c77a-5744-9802-65b0e97ef42c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{cos⁡x-2xsin⁡x}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327973} {:answer/id #uuid "85f38040-e7a5-59fd-a359-c862f85f6a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xcos⁡x-2sin⁡x}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328088} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c4ce4c-f609-552a-9123-d8fd254ccab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2xcos⁡x-xsin⁡x}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328396} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e8be1f-f487-5a62-b564-e74b12097ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xsin⁡x-2cos⁡x}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85f38040-e7a5-59fd-a359-c862f85f6a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xcos⁡x-2sin⁡x}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328088} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f86cadc-4440-532f-bf1a-6161f5f6bfb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3c4b5b5-b682-57e6-baf5-0601260b1ed0" :question/math-academy-id "q-47998" :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\frac{sin⁡x}{{x}^{2}},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=sin⁡x$ and $v(x)={x}^{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(sin⁡x)^{′}\\cdot {x}^{2}-sin⁡x\\cdot ({x}^{2})^{′}}{({x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}cos⁡x-2xsin⁡x}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(xcos⁡x-2sin⁡x)}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xcos⁡x-2sin⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83f86ab-a008-5487-b118-f1495a0fe4d6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016b59fa-7dd1-5430-8a0d-9d17b256b87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{e}^{4x-1}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326950} {:answer/id #uuid "1a09b33b-87d5-53a9-afee-f8e02962d4c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327160} {:answer/id #uuid "35f572ac-b945-560e-89f5-d45ed6858e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{e}^{4x-1}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327403} {:answer/id #uuid "bab77687-86e3-5b97-960a-d832566921c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{e}^{4x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328563} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa829b5-29ee-5ba4-a1c8-72e5faa0588a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{e}^{4x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfa829b5-29ee-5ba4-a1c8-72e5faa0588a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{e}^{4x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328611} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2f4dd66-f6d3-5f50-8ce9-b004c416733f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd0c521a-06b7-559f-8b78-1152ec8cbcd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-48007" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=-2{e}^{4x-1}+3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=-2{e}^{u}+3,\\,u=4x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-2{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{u}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-8{e}^{4x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d495799e-2f9d-5146-a399-506d9d32ad84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c9ba1e63c665ad63597c822828fdf26ec0fefade083df5ea828f57a49d3aa149.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392604} {:answer/id #uuid "c796e42f-5eb7-5d3a-900e-6d5fb58916e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fc/fc1d428500558ed62fbf5f7e0c13604cbc759629cedb63a8f9c2f7fc0b412480.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392605} {:answer/id #uuid "742220de-47f9-5f30-8679-f64626ac489f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d9/d9185fb471c9806d490133c6ea5a54ef69878f89ca0e0be66008ec3626bfe346.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392606} {:answer/id #uuid "11ce7ab1-ca48-5c69-b7f7-b7ff62f3ea3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/49/49e7b3c3cd4e6410b95a307f74c90079ec61c2d3540f86a357db88c77039ca44.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392607} {:answer/id #uuid "c37ded1b-1a1e-52ed-a977-31d60b87248f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bd/bd662a57e07bf3f0319a00a15dc5c520a172d59672eae24f7999a4535da0b9ee.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c796e42f-5eb7-5d3a-900e-6d5fb58916e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fc/fc1d428500558ed62fbf5f7e0c13604cbc759629cedb63a8f9c2f7fc0b412480.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392605} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac8479b0-0f39-5a85-82ad-a7d9ee04b2a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3d5f3e1-0f7f-56ba-9eea-f720be7a7122" :question/math-academy-id "q-48038" :question/problem "Select the function that has a discontinuity at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is shown below. Notice that the function contains a break exactly at $x=1$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n![](images/3c/3c8a198c1ec835590a3d755b964929ef9ac9a30e4268b2fdc80dacdb394899d8.png)\n\nAll the other given graphs can be plotted without taking the pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee867b9-645a-512d-9f99-f3aa8226ad71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327063} {:answer/id #uuid "4558acbf-a492-5e1c-acde-0aa26d25d7f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327540} {:answer/id #uuid "a58a5f87-8e65-5592-8409-a19f0dfcd0ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328364} {:answer/id #uuid "b013ab09-8aad-54e4-9a17-87cdc1aa5d04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328460} {:answer/id #uuid "c6636203-3372-5d9c-9639-986cdeaf7e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ee867b9-645a-512d-9f99-f3aa8226ad71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327063} :answer-field/id #uuid "e45de7c6-c5ce-5b9d-a7b8-ea5254d09158" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96069899-bca3-51de-b64c-6e29072ea13e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48062" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9db7f2a491ae3065dbe8434a71d378c4fed8fec16c82e2fd76dcb209c786809.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ exists\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function is not even defined at $x=1.$ The graph contains a break at $x=1.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f05bd8f-eb2f-57d7-ae18-c11817bd78b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327620} {:answer/id #uuid "83335e31-f97c-5df4-863a-ef88150fb6f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328065} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9a91aa-3ed8-5151-bcb5-e59ccd13d6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328272} {:answer/id #uuid "ce9e8871-1f30-5744-8374-042f56698b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328750} {:answer/id #uuid "d6472410-aa22-5e41-a0c0-09e47c3fb93c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83335e31-f97c-5df4-863a-ef88150fb6f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328065} :answer-field/id #uuid "23e84077-b88d-5119-bcf7-6c45cbfd0278" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e01edc7-98f4-52fe-bf43-cb1e05ae0d4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48065" :question/problem "![](images/fa/fa15192923f9fd7370818fcb6b05b24b3bb6901dfbf90dd53539d71fd0f8b0bf.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=f(2)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$ and $f(2)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=1=f(2).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db808f48-6d0c-5b35-9342-b1817df65172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02cb37f5-2f1d-5582-9b0a-ba3d5ae84781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326967} {:answer/id #uuid "12f35963-b5fb-5ee8-a217-7821558518c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327097} {:answer/id #uuid "29f970a0-530a-5098-9cef-2daea001ef9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327295} {:answer/id #uuid "39dad4a5-4110-5374-97bc-fe41c7c0f5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5x-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327440} {:answer/id #uuid "83d0f077-ab57-54f6-80e1-8b63e8a4c95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83d0f077-ab57-54f6-80e1-8b63e8a4c95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328072} :answer-field/id #uuid "324d4519-c59d-5772-95b5-cc69664e41ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5a7a5a2-50a1-5b97-8ac0-810e9082e127" :question/math-academy-id "q-4807" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=2{x}^{2}+x$ at the point $(1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+x)=4x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1)=4(1)+1=5\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=5$ and the coordinates of our point $(1,3),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(3) & =5(x-1) \\\\ y & =5x-5+3 \\\\ y & =5x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5cffd7b-e09a-5e8f-9bc6-d30e9bf80623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c6/c6f0914f0b218164bc99fdefe7d83dffe4591a6b704e2243013d05c2b117ef17.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392617} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba0ee0f-2ced-5835-84e5-0ed53e1549d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c2/c2f85d1a2becc7bfe6f71d7f6fcb178f4707f4b41956015eabb38610de95feca.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392618} {:answer/id #uuid "d5853982-4adc-5148-b192-1f951c92f732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/14349b3dee80a6d451efedc55b91b5e709139f1a93fabf539d605843513f6141.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392619} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c69f46-c3ba-5974-ae7f-6acba3a9675b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/30/3073f651e0194c8675629f8fc52d45694a64680e3208b8fd7ef066e38d715033.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392620} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c4e630-276e-54fd-922f-6f7389462a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c95d9944eb569c36d0b546257b5fde5a2d8c2aa8984eb98d553df832c2b1989f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5cffd7b-e09a-5e8f-9bc6-d30e9bf80623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c6/c6f0914f0b218164bc99fdefe7d83dffe4591a6b704e2243013d05c2b117ef17.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392617} :answer-field/id #uuid "9780e3c9-7665-5af4-99ca-1678d1cb66a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b614a15-6e39-5d82-8606-db6fffa56cff" :question/math-academy-id "q-48082" :question/problem "Select the function that is continuous at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is shown below.  Indeed, despite the fact that the function has a discontinuity at $x=2$, the graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n![](images/90/90c2fbe99d0b202e09244084d2501608018e030e6660a225f708cbc6b462dccd.png)\n\n   \n\nAll the other given graphs contain breaks at $x=0$ and it would be impossible to draw them in a neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking the pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f8cb089-6505-515d-b264-99e427a1221e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396675} {:answer/id #uuid "c08b5c35-a5d7-5069-b56f-c620adb60e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396676} {:answer/id #uuid "86dd1c08-dfcc-5440-8ed6-a1599aed6c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396677} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a54622-a441-5f4f-8a9d-6f28f972e33c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396678} {:answer/id #uuid "50484ca8-86cd-5313-806f-14cb83956a21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f8cb089-6505-515d-b264-99e427a1221e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396675} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d5e1e7b-8e38-5b41-953d-3eb5f39cc12b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6e088c5-8b59-5061-af03-e9b40ee3d2a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-48088" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e111be8806fd5351b873219d9a790b71ca862a8fdef8b15df6477265cbb7ddea.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" :question/worked-solution "The graph breaks at $x=-3$ and $x=0$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-3$ and $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078618 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c538b0a9-a084-5a83-a5fa-eca4e0270a93" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing an Exponential Function and a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5591b8c8-3047-562b-8ef4-8361d55d25b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327673} {:answer/id #uuid "5c302237-b94a-5fe0-81b1-2587947f173d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327742} {:answer/id #uuid "86671781-4a8f-573e-8fe7-aaff83f7332d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{e}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328093} {:answer/id #uuid "ad75e9f7-6e31-5997-8593-a8ef1838747e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328440} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b4ca71-189f-58de-8fcc-5367fa284f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad75e9f7-6e31-5997-8593-a8ef1838747e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328440} :answer-field/id #uuid "314cef58-95bb-572e-a378-8e9c798f6f07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb78782d-135f-57ce-a3ed-0b840f2e8d22" :question/math-academy-id "q-48212" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x){e}^{-x}+5).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x){e}^{-x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x){e}^{-x}+5) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}+5) \\\\  & =0+5 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078617 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "433ac237-32ff-591c-ab0d-e271f5beda30" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing a Polynomial Function and a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "385935e5-40b2-5cdf-a2a7-ac3e7865d543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327426} {:answer/id #uuid "814d202d-efc4-5b86-b450-3050ed558ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328048} {:answer/id #uuid "afe78bc0-6b9b-58bb-a6b3-3d1ea65b8862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218