 :knowledge-point/title "Determining Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "225115ed-fe14-54a2-9ff7-14b01a6ca201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398850} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c706c6-a7cb-5cbf-a31c-59d0e18e0111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398851} {:answer/id #uuid "0db6826b-749e-538b-898e-9f8e45963932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398852} {:answer/id #uuid "b3965102-b7bf-58c1-9626-978cbd7ed7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398853} {:answer/id #uuid "b32f9c1b-732e-5499-a950-ae4e40e8d8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9c706c6-a7cb-5cbf-a31c-59d0e18e0111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398851} :answer-field/id #uuid "134c28b1-b3ff-5d06-b647-ccf5e9b6bae2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b5e7d86-6590-5293-af9e-465db1c9211b" :question/math-academy-id "q-48234" :question/problem "![](images/43/43786cb577705d6ae13d44e75203fef441905e9ccfc3713726f07d36c147c7b3.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity. \n\nThe function is defined on the interval $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The function is discontinuous at $x=-3$ and at $x=3,$ so we need to exclude these points..\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,3),$ and $(-3,\\infty ).$  It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following: \n\n$\\,$ $(-\\infty ,-3)$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $(-4,-2)$ and $(-\\infty ,1)$ contain the excluded point $-3,$  so the function is not continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $(-2,4)$ and $(2,\\infty )$ contain the excluded point $3,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36524faf-ac61-5756-89e3-c83a947ac61b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Closed Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "918d5704-c936-55d5-8203-eac0ffb33b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/72/72d7fd3effd14aa4449bae360aabd3577846653b5ec364933356e5ba40499cae.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076719} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0d0ca5-e6b9-5a9b-9739-4d0d30653d75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b5/b58a88020b68d05c001a188faf7f3c4b1f1b57f8f9a9854d86ec2dc44b6bc347.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076720} {:answer/id #uuid "efa4479b-f8c0-5b64-9dd7-acfb425f9f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/97/978bb8f022b5a8ff96a47c79c5db11f455511addfaea55cb27f92ccc1c133072.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076721} {:answer/id #uuid "004d454c-d47a-5a5b-a963-7444ffec7a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/